From 2f9bf81ab3ba5f2ca8daecda5fb66b6995ff6373 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ObliviOS Build <build@localhost>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 19:31:19 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] oblivios: let one enrolled finger open the Duress Unlock
 decoy

A finger chosen in Settings opens the decoy at the keyguard. The biometric
stack (AuthenticationClient) asks LockSettingsInternal.routeDecoyFinger for
every fingerprint match before any token reaches the keystore. For the
duress finger at the keyguard, DecoyProfileHelper switches to the decoy
through the same mint as the typed decoy credential, using the decoy
credential kept by DecoyFingerHelper (wrapped under a key derived from the
real user's synthetic password, unwrapped into system_server memory when
the real credential is verified). The keyguard gets a success for no user,
so the real session is neither dismissed nor trusted and its keystore stays
unauthorised. The duress finger anywhere else (a BiometricPrompt, or with
no decoy, no cached credential or biometrics not allowed) is an unrecognised
finger and never opens the real session. DecoyFingerPolicy and
DecoyFingerCodec are pure and tested on the JVM. setDecoyFinger,
clearDecoyFinger and getDecoyFingerId are appended last to ILockSettings and
need the write permission.

Not compiled: the services module has no host-side type-check here.
